WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.730
- I am here with lifestyle expert

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.657
and green living guru, Danny Seo.

00:00:05.657 --> 00:00:06.490
(audience applauding)

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:07.880
Who, by the way, I'm starting to think

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.710
maybe we were separated at birth.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:11.910
Because I believe I'm so interested

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:13.650
in so many of the things you discuss,

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.205
and live, and are true in your life.

00:00:16.205 --> 00:00:18.410
So what are we gonna be doing here?

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:19.243
- What we're doing is,

00:00:19.243 --> 00:00:21.480
we're going to up-cycle a children's book

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.633
into this beautiful storybook bird house.

00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:25.466
- [Drew] I absolutely love it.

00:00:25.466 --> 00:00:27.495
- [Danny] It looks complicated,
it's So not complicated.

00:00:27.495 --> 00:00:28.328
- Okay, show me how it's done, Danny.

00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:31.460
- So first step, this is
like $2 at a craft store.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.900
It's just a regular
unfinished, wooden bird house.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.540
- By the way, which bird
is fitting in there?

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:41.120
Why are bird house holes so small?

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.500
Does the bird become liquid
when it goes through these?

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.260
Because I don't get it.
And it's every bird house.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.850
The openings are literally
the size of a quarter.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:51.770
- [Danny] I think they can fit.

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:52.603
- How?

00:00:52.603 --> 00:00:53.436
- I think they're small.

00:00:53.436 --> 00:00:55.537
You know, we're not talking
about pigeons or seagulls.

00:00:55.537 --> 00:01:00.519
- But it's like every bird
house has really small holes.

00:01:00.519 --> 00:01:01.741
- You know...

00:01:01.741 --> 00:01:03.888
I will, I will look into that.

00:01:03.888 --> 00:01:04.721
(everyone laughing)

00:01:04.721 --> 00:01:06.750
- I need to know because
every time I stare at it,

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:07.950
it gives me anxiety.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.520
Like, do they have to force
themselves through there?

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:11.690
Okay, thank you, Danny.

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:13.610
Help me 'cause it does give me anxiety.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:15.540
- We're gonna do something
called decoupaging.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:16.900
What you do is you take a book,

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.160
I've already taken all
the pages out of the book.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:22.650
And you just, sloppily,
put on as much glue

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.380
as possible, all over it.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.304
- And is this the special decoupage glue?

00:01:26.304 --> 00:01:27.137
- It is.

00:01:27.137 --> 00:01:29.003
So what you do, is you
take the page you want,

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.720
just plop it through, go
right through the hole.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.720
Use your fingers to actually
get the paper on, like this.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:40.390
And then what you do,

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:43.100
is that the more glue you get on,

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.400
the more it's gonna seal it.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.640
And the more it's going
to really get the pages

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.840
stuck on here,

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.510
- [Drew] Okay. So then
we do the glue on top?

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.530
- On top too, 'cause you
really want to saturate it.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:54.770
'Cause it really, then it sticks.

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:56.880
And when it dries, it seals it too.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.130
So while it looks really
messy and sloppy right now,

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:02.463
this glue dries to a perfect
finish every single time.

00:02:02.463 --> 00:02:03.296
- Really?

00:02:03.296 --> 00:02:04.580
- And then the fun part
is, if you got the kids,

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.020
'cause you got that little you know,

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:07.770
that hole at the top right here?

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:08.603
It's like, how do you do that?

00:02:08.603 --> 00:02:11.810
You just boop, stick your
finger right through it.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:12.897
And then take that extra paper...

00:02:12.897 --> 00:02:14.390
- I don't think I can get
my finger, oh, there we go.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.863
- Look at that. You're
already like two seconds.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:21.170
We're already getting this
bird house, like on its way.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.670
And then you just go around and around

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:24.990
and keep adding more glue.

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.534
And then you layer the paper on top.

00:02:27.534 --> 00:02:28.367
- Okay.

00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:31.020
- If you don't like, let's
say you don't want to do this

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.390
because it's already saturated.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:33.600
You can just tear it.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.560
You see how it just naturally tearing

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.757
into the shape of the bird house?

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:39.390
And you go around and around like this

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:42.760
until you're actually
finished with the birdhouse.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:46.430
What you could do, if you
only have one bird house,

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:48.340
and you're doing let's say
a kid's party or something,

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.490
you could also cut out all the characters.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.430
- Oh, I like that.

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:54.280
- And start layering and
create a little story book

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:55.113
on here too.

00:02:55.113 --> 00:02:57.620
Or if you don't want to cover
the whole thing in the pages,

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:59.920
you could paint it first

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.500
and then actually create little stories

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.080
of your own with the characters.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.590
- Oh, that's cute. Like
a story book, bird house.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:07.140
- [Danny] That's exactly it.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.450
- [Drew] Oh, I love that.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:10.807
- And what I love is that
the cost of something

00:03:10.807 --> 00:03:13.550
like this is a few dollars.

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.740
For this effect, you
don't have to do the roof,

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:19.929
because your actual book
jacket becomes the roof.

00:03:19.929 --> 00:03:22.210
- [Drew] Oh.
- [Danny] You see?

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:23.670
And then you really just
go around and around

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.170
and keep adding things
like this until you have...

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:28.830
(audience applauding)

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.613
- A beautiful storybook bird house.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:35.510
Now Danny, here's the thing,

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:37.840
mine's never going to look like yours.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.230
Mine's gonna look like
this, which is, you know...

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:44.420
- I think there's a
lot of beauty in truth.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:46.289
The truth is you can't make it.

00:03:46.289 --> 00:03:48.715
(everyone laughing)

00:03:48.715 --> 00:03:51.132
- It's so true. It's so true.

00:03:52.981 --> 00:03:54.541
- [Danny] Just so you know,

00:03:54.541 --> 00:03:56.160
this project does not work if
you use pages of my magazine.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:57.120
It's very weird.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:57.953
- Really?

00:03:57.953 --> 00:04:00.070
Yeah. It just, it just
rejects it completely.

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:04.720
- It's like a glossy paper
stock that won't be decoupaged?

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:06.775
- No, I just don't want people
tearing apart my magazine.

00:04:06.775 --> 00:04:09.144
(everyone laughing)
- [Drew] Oh, okay.

00:04:09.144 --> 00:04:10.203
Well, I shan't.

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:13.390
I shan't maim your magazine.

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:15.730
And I just thank you so much, Danny.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:18.050
And be sure to pick up your copy

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:21.970
of "Naturally Danny
Seo" on newsstands now.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:23.300
It's quarterly, right?

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:24.133
- [Danny] Quarterly, yep.

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:25.218
- Thank you, Danny.

00:04:25.218 --> 00:04:26.552
- [Danny] Thank you, Drew.

00:04:26.552 --> 00:04:27.780
- Thank you, guys. This was so fun.

00:04:29.194 --> 00:04:32.361
(audience applauding)

